{
  "requirement": "TBV owns the model conversation, supported backend integrations, tool\ndispatch, and structured submission used by its ask builder.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I judge the tests adequate for `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.tool_agent`. The requirement is a broad ownership statement. TBV owns the model conversation, the supported backend integrations, tool dispatch, and structured submission used by the ask builder. Finer obligations sit in refining requirements: `.isolation`, `.provider_failure`, `submission.*`, `fixed_tools` and the config requirements. Those have their own tests, and I did not reassess them here.\n\n**Model conversation and structured submission**\n- `agent/tests.rs#fn[behavioral_adequacy_uses_domain_contents_and_returns_a_typed_fingerprinted_answer]` drives a `ToolAgent` through the ask path. It checks the system and user messages sent to the provider and the `submit_result` tool schema. It also checks that the typed answer is returned.\n- `fn[concurrent_tool_agents_isolate_histories_pages_and_submissions_and_share_reads]` and `fn[validator_agent_executions_isolate_conversations_and_dependency_traces]` run concurrent conversations. They assert per-conversation call counts (3 each), the right answer for each, and the recorded dependencies.\n\n**Tool dispatch**\n- `fn[application_tools_preserve_graph_tools_in_the_same_conversation]` offers graph tools, an application tool and `submit_result` in one conversation. It asserts the tool list on every request. It asserts that tool results are delivered in order, including paging, and that the dependencies are recorded.\n- `fn[application_tool_factories_create_fresh_state_for_each_agent_call]` checks that application tool state is fresh for each call.\n- `fn[source_tools_keep_search_discovery_separate_from_delivered_source_reads]` dispatches the source tools (`search_text`, `read_source`) and checks the recorded observation.\n\n**Supported backend integrations** (`AgentConfig` has Bedrock, OpenAI and Ollama)\n- `configured.rs#fn[supplied_openai_backend_runs_independent_calls_against_a_local_transport]` runs against a local HTTP server. It asserts the request path, the model and `max_output_tokens`, and the exact tool list and `submit_result` schema. It asserts that a tool result is fed back on the second turn. It asserts that the config's secrets (API key and model name) are not persisted. It runs two independent calls.\n- `fn[supplied_ollama_backend_delivers_tools_and_results_over_native_chat]` does the same over native chat. It asserts the `/api/chat` path, the bearer key, `stream=false`, the options and the submit schema. It asserts that the tool result reaches the next message.\n- Bedrock has no end-to-end conversation test against a local transport. Its backend-specific logic is tested directly. The marked tests are `providers.rs#fn[bedrock_native_option_types_are_checked_and_only_max_tokens_is_defaulted]`, `fn[cache_checkpoints_are_requested_for_caching_models]` and `fn[typed_client_calls_preserve_order_and_native_history]`. The last checks that tool-call order and native history are preserved. The same file also tests credentials, retries and SDK config. The generic conversation, dispatch and submission loop that Bedrock shares with the other backends is covered by the OpenAI, Ollama and `ToolAgent` tests above. The text does not require a per-backend conversation test. I do not treat the missing Bedrock transport test as an established gap.\n\nI found no obligation in the subject text that lacks a test with a relevant assertion. The tests carry the `{test}` reference to the subject. No acknowledgments apply.",
